 "scenes": [
  {
   "id": "intro",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A binary search tree is fast only while it stays short, and sorted input makes it a chain. The AVL tree, the first self-balancing tree, checks its balance after every change and repairs it with rotations. This video shows how."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "head"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "AVL trees"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Here are the eight stages."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "stages"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The balance factor, why AVL trees stay short, and single and double rotations."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[0..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Then two worked insertion sequences, deletion and costs, and an exam question."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[4..=7]"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "bf",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 1: the balance factor of a node is the height of its left subtree minus the height of its right subtree."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 1 of 8: the balance factor"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "stages"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "bf"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "bf[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "An empty subtree has height minus 1, so a leaf has height 0 and balance factor 0."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "bf[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "An AVL tree keeps every node's balance factor at minus 1, 0 or plus 1: the two subtrees of any node differ in height by at most one."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "bf[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "An insertion or deletion can push some node to plus or minus 2. Then a rotation restores the rule."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "bf[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "balance factor ∈ {−1, 0, +1} at every node"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "height",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 2: why does this keep the tree short? Ask the opposite: what is the fewest nodes an AVL tree of height h can have?"
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 2 of 8: why AVL trees stay short"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "bf"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "hb"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hb[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The sparsest tree of height h has a root, one subtree of height h minus 1, and one of height h minus 2, both as sparse as possible."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "reveal",
       "target": "hb[1]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hb[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "That gives 1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 20: a Fibonacci-like sequence, growing exponentially in h."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "reveal",
       "target": "hb[2]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hb[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Turn it round: the height is less than 1.44 log 2 of n plus 2. A million keys give a height of at most 29, where a plain BST could reach 999,999."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "reveal",
       "target": "hb[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hb[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "AVL height < 1.44 log₂ n"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "single",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 3: rotations. Find the lowest node that became unbalanced, and look at where the new key went. Four cases."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 3 of 8: single rotations"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "hb"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "rt"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rt[0..=1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "LL: the key went into the left subtree of the left child. One right rotation fixes it. RR is the mirror image: one left rotation."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rt[0..=1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A left rotation at x: y, x's right child, moves up. y's left subtree moves across to become x's right subtree. x becomes y's left child."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "rt"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "rr"
      },
      {
       "action": "mark_lines",
       "target": "rr",
       "from": 1,
       "to": 5
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Only three pointers change, and the inorder order of the keys is untouched: it is still a search tree."
     },
     "do": [
      {
